<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<!-- The floor and the newest, and nothing between: a parser here is ordinary C#
		     over a generated one, so netstandard2.0 is what every consumer can read and
		     net10.0 is what a consumer on the newest runtime gets without the shims. -->
		<TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
		<RootNamespace>DotGram.Sql</RootNamespace>

		<Description>SQL parsers written in .gram, building one tree of records: the ISO standard's SQL-92 and Microsoft's T-SQL.</Description>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageTags>parser;sql;iso-sql;tsql;t-sql;sql-server;dotgram</PackageTags>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>The trees and their writers are unchanged, and so is every method that was here before: what moved is what the grammars read, and there is one more way to ask.

Every publication now has a third method, bool TryParseX(string input, out T value), beside ParseX and the Match&lt;T&gt; form. It answers only whether the text reads, says nothing about what was expected where it does not, and a refusal costs it about half, since nothing is read a second time to record the message.

SqlStandardParser reads the standard through a lexical split, which bounds two things the ISO grammar leaves unbounded: a comment holds others six deep, and a /* inside a comment always opens one. It now reads what it used to refuse — a datetime less a datetime in brackets before an interval qualifier, a JSON behaviour and its ON clause, and the JSON and period forms of a WHEN operand — and refuses what it used to take, where an operand would swallow a reading that fits the text better. Sql92Parser follows it where the two share a production.

The package still ships no XML documentation, so an editor shows no summaries for the tree's types: what each node is named after is in docs/ast.md, and how to use the parsers is in the README and the SKILL beside it. It is a debt of this package rather than a decision about its API.

TransactSqlParser leaves a statement's label to that statement: HIDDEN, SORTED_DATA and a login's password words no longer take the label on the line after them. A native module's atomic block is now followed by the end of the batch, said as such, rather than by a refusal naming no statement.</PackageReleaseNotes>

		<!-- Symbols inside the assembly, as the generator carries them: a stack trace from
		     a parser here has line numbers without a symbol server. -->
		<DebugType>embedded</DebugType>

		<!-- Paths in the PDB and the package normalized to the repository rather than the
		     runner's disk, so that what CI packs can be rebuilt byte for byte from the commit
		     its nuspec names. Here and not in Directory.Build.props: it rewrites every source
		     path, [CallerFilePath] included, to /_/, and nine test classes find the
		     repository from their own file — set for everything, it failed eighty of them.
		     Only on CI, because locally it would point a debugger at /_/. -->
		<ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>

		<!-- Generated files land on disk so a reader can open what the grammars became. -->
		<EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>true</EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>
		<CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>$(BaseIntermediateOutputPath)GeneratedFiles</CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The generator, and nothing else: a .gram parser acquires no runtime
		     dependency, which is why this can be an ordinary library (docs/syntax.md
		     §6.1). -->
		<ProjectReference Include="..\DotGram\DotGram.csproj"
		                  OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
		<!-- What a consumer on this framework needs: the generated parser's own methods
		     take `ReadOnlySpan<char>`, which is not in the framework there. It is the one
		     dependency this package has, and it has it only here. -->
		<PackageReference Include="System.Memory" />

		<!-- And what the compilation needs rather than the consumer: the records written
		     by hand beside the grammars have `init` accessors, and `IsExternalInit` is not
		     declared on this framework. The polyfill is a generator and emits it as an
		     internal type, so nothing reaches the package. -->
		<PackageReference Include="Meziantou.Polyfill" PrivateAssets="all" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
		<!-- `required` members, which the SQL:2023 tree's records declare, need two more. -->
		<MeziantouPolyfill_IncludedPolyfills>T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.IsExternalInit;T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RequiredMemberAttribute;T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.CompilerFeatureRequiredAttribute;T:System.Index;T:System.Range;T:System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis</MeziantouPolyfill_IncludedPolyfills>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- What the package says for itself. The grammars are in the repository and the
		     README points at them: a parser here is worth reading as much as it is worth
		     calling. -->
		<None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />

		<!-- And the skill beside it, so that an agent in a project that has restored this
		     package can read how to use it without leaving the machine. The README says
		     where it is, in a comment nothing renders. -->
		<None Include="SKILL.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The grammars as files the generator reads: a .gram beside the class that hosts
		     it, so that another project can point the generator at the same text and compile
		     it another way — which is what DotGram.Benchmarks does with the SQL grammar. -->
		<AdditionalFiles Include="Standard\SqlStandard.gram" />
		<AdditionalFiles Include="Standard\SqlStandard92.gram" />
		<AdditionalFiles Include="TransactSql\TransactSql.gram" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
